Usages:
  conventions: .goga/usages/conventions.md
  prettyplay: .goga/usages/cooks/prettyplay.md
  allure-pytest: .goga/usages/cooks/allure-pytest.md

Annotations: |
  Use `conventions` for code writing rules, relative imports, docstrings, and testing.
  Use `prettyplay` for the prettyplay contract — the PrettyConfig field table, PrettyPlay construction
  (cache_key/cache_path/config), the StepHooks event set, and the security rules.
  Use `allure-pytest` for the guarded import and the StepHooks-to-Allure mapping with failure
  screenshots.

  Web-library cell — the prettyplay test kind's library surface: the settings materialization of the
  validated web config into the complete effective prettyplay settings (no mirror model — the cell
  declares no config models of its own and consumes the already validated activation), the Allure
  step reporting hooks, and the console event formatter. The web config key set and the document
  validation live in the plugins cell (the WebPlugin reads and validates web.yml into the library
  PrettyConfig at configphase).
  web.yml is the single configuration source for web tests (the single CLI exception: the typed
  --browser-endpoint flag of the plugins cell overrides browser.endpoint on the document copy
  before the validation — never a second document, never an env source): the web fixture
  passes the explicitly
  built config to the runtime, and prettyplay adopts an explicit config verbatim — the library's
  pyproject+env file layer never loads for it and never back-fills anything, so an empty or omitted
  key keeps the library model default. The env layer never reaches the runtime either way: configure
  scrubs the PRETTYPLAY_* variables of the process once at configphase (a process-level guard on
  top of the verbatim adoption). The library's pyproject auto-search runs only on the config-less
  file-layer construction the web fixture never takes — a repository without a pyproject.toml
  upward from the cwd no longer fails web tests at fixture setup. The library's semantic
  validators (the browser matrix, the endpoint scheme — ws/wss or http/https of the CDP family, the
  speed range) run at the plugins-cell activation — a
  semantic violation fails the run at configphase, before any collection or browser. LLM API keys
  live only in env/.env (the root load_env loads it);
  web.yml never carries keys; step sentences, captured vars, and cache files land in LLM prompts and
  logs — no secrets in step sentences (documented in this cell's consumer usage doc).
  The pytest integration (the WebPlugin: the web fixture, the strict/interactive options, the web
  config key set, and the document validation) lives in
  the plugins cell and is activated by its installation gate on the presence of the web config
  document; this cell registers nothing on its own. Web steps carry full URLs
  (web.step("Open https://ya.ru")) — no sentence templating, no sandbox binding; the tested
  site's own base URL is the site.base_url directive of web.yml (the site group of the library
  PrettyConfig), carried by the web runtime and consumed by this cell's BasePage.

  The page-object base: BasePage binds a web runtime and a page path the way the api kind's
  Endpoint binds an Api and a route path — the base URL lives on the runtime
  (PrettyPlay.site_base_url, see `prettyplay`), the page adds its path, and the page performs
  its characteristic action — opening the full URL through one ordinary action step sentence —
  on call, the Endpoint pattern; the navigation participates in the step cycle (cache,
  generation, healing) like every authored step. The step surface (action, assertion, group)
  is proxied onto the page, so tests and page methods alike drive the runtime without the .web
  hop; the runtime itself stays public for everything else (run_on_page, site_base_url). The
  context manager scopes the work on an opened page under a short local name — the exit closes
  nothing (the runtime lifecycle belongs to the web fixture of the plugins cell) and never
  suppresses. Test pages are subclasses declaring a class-level path attribute — the working
  pattern of a real page — and a subclass also encapsulates the page's own scenario logic as
  methods (closing a cookie banner, a form flow) running their step sentences through the
  proxied surface; a per-page fixture constructs the subclass with the runtime, the mirror of
  the api kind's Endpoint(api, path) fixtures.

  The cell also carries the per-test prompt declaration surface: the `generation_prompt`
  decorator (the pattern of the sandbox services decorator) attaches the
  pybuggy_generation_prompt marker and a usefixtures("web") stamp to a test function — a pure
  declaration; this cell registers nothing on its own. The marker registration and the per-test
  read live in the plugins cell (the WebPlugin configure step and the web fixture). Decorator
  text, like step sentences, lands in LLM prompts and logs — the no-secrets rule extends to it
  (documented in this cell's consumer usage doc).

  Constraints:
  - The cell declares no config models of its own — the document model is the library PrettyConfig,
    imported from prettyplay and never re-exported by the facade.
  - Use relative imports inside the cell.
  - The only direct playwright import is playwright.sync_api.Error (a transitive dependency, not
    declared separately — documented in `prettyplay`).
  - allure-pytest is never a runtime dependency: the import sits under a guard (allure is None
    constructs no hooks — web tests run without step decoration).

---

"build_pretty_config(config: PrettyConfig, strict_flag: bool, interactive_flag: bool, prompt_extra: str = \"\") -> pretty: PrettyConfig":
  location: pretty.py
  annotations: |
    Materialize the validated web config with the resolved strict and interactive modes and
    the per-test prompt extras — the single construction point of the prettyplay settings
    layer.

    `config`: the validated web config (the activation).
    `strict_flag`: the effective strict override — True forces strict even when the document says
      false.
    `interactive_flag`: the effective interactive override — the same form.
    `prompt_extra`: the per-test extra generation instructions (the decorator texts, already
      joined by the caller); empty means no extras.
    `pretty`: the explicitly constructed prettyplay config.

    Algorithm:
    1. Compute the effective modes: strict = config.strict or strict_flag; interactive =
       config.interactive or interactive_flag — a CLI flag forces the mode on, never off.
    2. Compute the effective generation prompt: join the non-empty parts of
       config.generation_prompt and `prompt_extra`, in this order, with a single newline.
    3. Construct the explicit object — every field, the browser group included, passed as a
       constructor argument; generation_prompt receives the computed join.

    Requirements:
    - The all-fields construction is load-bearing: every field lands in the constructed model,
      and the runtime adopts the result verbatim; an empty or None field keeps the library
      model default (see `prettyplay`).
    - Pure — no I/O, no environment reads.
    - `prompt_extra` defaults to the empty string — call sites without extras are unchanged.

    Constraints:
    - Do not read env or pyproject here — the single source is the passed model and flags.
    - Do not normalize `prompt_extra` beyond the empty-part skip — the texts are stripped and
      joined by the caller.

    Use `prettyplay` for the PrettyConfig and browser config field contract.
    Use `conventions` for type hints.

"generation_prompt(text: str) -> decorator: Callable":
  location: pretty.py
  annotations: |
    Per-test generation-instruction decorator: declare test-specific extra instructions that
    append (newline-joined) to the generation prompt of exactly this test — web.yml stays the
    single global source, the decorator carries only the extras (see `prettyplay` for the
    generation_prompt field the text rides on).

    `text`: the instruction text — a non-empty, non-whitespace-only string.
    `decorator`: the marker decorator applied to a test function.

    Algorithm:
    1. Validate at decoration: `text` must be a str whose stripped form is non-empty — a
       non-string, empty, or whitespace-only value raises ValueError (visible at collection,
       before any browser or LLM).
    2. Attach the pybuggy_generation_prompt marker carrying the stripped text as its `text`
       kwarg.
    3. Stamp the function with a usefixtures mark requesting the web fixture — the marked test
       always gets the web runtime, with or without an explicit web parameter.
    4. Return the function unchanged.

    Requirements:
    - Function-level application; stacking allowed — each application attaches its own marker,
      and the texts concatenate in the order written (the plugins-cell web fixture collects
      them).
    - Pure declaration — no I/O, no plugin lookup, no config read; works without web.yml (the
      failure then arrives at setup as pytest's standard fixture-not-found).

    Constraints:
    - Do not wrap the function body and do not execute anything at decoration time.
    - No new config keys and no presets — the web.yml key set stays fixed.

    Use `conventions` for docstrings, type hints, and testing.
    Use `prettyplay` for the generation_prompt semantics and the no-secrets rule.

"BasePage(web: PrettyPlay, path: str = \"\")":
  location: pages.py
  annotations: |
    The page-object base of the web test kind: one tested-site page — the web runtime plus a
    path, the web kind's counterpart of the api kind's Endpoint.

    `web`: the web runtime of the current test — the site.base_url source (the site_base_url
    property, see `prettyplay`) and the step executor of the navigation; held as the public
    `web` attribute the way Endpoint holds its api (run_on_page and everything beyond the step
    surface stay there).
    `path`: the page path resolved against the site base URL; empty falls back to the `path`
    class attribute of the subclass — the declaration form class LoginPage(BasePage): path =
    "/login"; the default class attribute is the empty string (the site root).

    Algorithm:
    1. Hold the runtime; resolve the effective path — the constructor argument wins over the
       class attribute (an empty argument falls back to it).
    2. url joins: site.base_url + "/" + path with the joining slashes normalized (a trailing
       slash of the base and a leading slash of the path never duplicate); an empty path yields
       the bare base URL; an unset base URL raises the loud actionable PrettyplayError naming
       the site.base_url directive of web.yml.
    3. The activation call executes one action step sentence "Open <url>" through the runtime
       and returns the page itself — the characteristic action on call, the Endpoint pattern;
       an ordinary step of the cycle: generated once, cached, replayed, healed.
    4. step, expect and group proxy their sentences to the bound runtime verbatim — the page's
       own surface for tests and scenario methods alike, the keyword-only parameters passed
       through unchanged.
    5. The context manager returns the page from enter; the exit is scoping only — it closes
       nothing and never suppresses (a falsy return keeps the block exception propagating).

    Requirements:
    - The URL join is pure — no I/O, no config reads beyond the runtime property.
    - The activation call delegates wholly to the runtime's action step — no direct playwright
      navigation, no page pre-opening (the page opens lazily on the first step, see
      `prettyplay`); the call is never overridden — scenario logic lives in named methods.
    - step, expect and group are reserved names of the page surface — a subclass never
      redefines them.
    - A missing site.base_url fails loudly at first use (url or the call), never at
      construction — a BasePage over an unset site works as a plain path holder.
    - The runtime lifecycle belongs to the web fixture of the plugins cell — the context
      manager manages nothing on exit.

    Constraints:
    - No new config keys here — the base URL arrives through the validated activation carried
      by the runtime (the plugins cell owns web.yml).
    - No lifecycle management — no close, no teardown; the with-block is scoping only.
    - Do not proxy beyond the step surface — run_on_page and the rest of the runtime stay on
      the `web` attribute.
    - The full URL lands in the step sentence — the no-secrets rule of `prettyplay` covers the
      base URL and the path (no tokens in query strings).

    Use `prettyplay` for the step surface signatures, site_base_url, and the no-secrets rule.
    Use `conventions` for docstrings, type hints, and testing.
  properties:
    "web -> PrettyPlay": |
      The bound web runtime — the tested page's step executor and site.base_url source; the
      escape hatch for everything beyond the step surface (run_on_page).
    "path -> str": |
      The effective page path — the constructor argument or the subclass class attribute.
    "url -> str": |
      The full page URL — the site base URL with this page's path, slashes normalized; raises
      the loud actionable PrettyplayError when the site base URL is unset.
  methods:
    "step(text: str, tries: int | None, delay: float | None, vars: dict[str, str] | None)": |
      Execute one action step sentence through the bound runtime — the sentence and the
      keyword-only parameters passed through verbatim.
    "expect(text: str, tries: int | None, delay: float | None, vars: dict[str, str] | None)": |
      Execute one assertion step sentence through the bound runtime — the same pass-through
      form as the action proxy.
    "group(prompt: str, speed: int | None, delay: float | None) -> g: StepGroup": |
      Open the group authoring block through the bound runtime and yield its group object.
    "__call__() -> page: BasePage": |
      Open this page — one action step sentence carrying the full URL — and return the page
      itself: the activation call of the with-form (with page() as page:), the pattern of the
      api kind's Endpoint; never overridden — scenario logic lives in named methods.
    "__enter__() -> page: BasePage": |
      Enter the page block — the activation call has opened the page; yields this page as the
      short local name of the block.
    "__exit__(exc_type: object, exc_value: object, traceback: object)": |
      Leave the page block — scoping only: closes nothing (the runtime lifecycle belongs to
      the web fixture of the plugins cell) and never suppresses — the falsy return keeps the
      block exception propagating.

"AllureStepHooks(play: PrettyPlay)":
  location: allure_hooks.py
  annotations: |
    Allure reporting integration over the prettyplay hook contract — a StepHooks subclass active
    only when allure-pytest is installed (guarded import; see `allure-pytest`).

    `play`: the prettyplay runtime whose steps are reported — the failure screenshot source.

    Algorithm:
    1. Hold two exit stacks — steps and groups — so the enter/exit pairs stay balanced when a step
       fails mid-group (see `allure-pytest` for the stack discipline).

    Requirements:
    - Constructed only when the guarded allure import succeeded — never on import failure.

    Use `allure-pytest` for the mapping table and the stack discipline.
    Use `prettyplay` for the StepHooks event signatures and the screenshot source.
  methods:
    "on_group_started(group_prompt: str)": |
      Enter an allure step context titled "[group] " + group_prompt on the group stack.

      `group_prompt`: the group sentence.
    "on_group_finished(group_prompt: str)": |
      Close the group stack — every entered group context exits.

      `group_prompt`: the group sentence.
    "on_step_started(step_text: str, step_type: str)": |
      Enter an allure step wrapper titled "[<step_type>] <step_text>" on the step stack.

      `step_text`: the step sentence.
      `step_type`: the step type (action or assertion).
    "on_step_failed(step_text: str, step_type: str, error: str)": |
      Mark the current step failed and attach the failure screenshot: AssertionError for the
      assertion type, a generic Exception otherwise; the screenshot bytes attach as screenshot.png,
      and the capture is best-effort — a PlaywrightError of the capture and the library's no-page
      error (a PrettyplayError) are both swallowed: no page, no screenshot (the failure itself is
      already recorded; see `prettyplay` for the only playwright import).

      `step_text`: the step sentence.
      `step_type`: the step type.
      `error`: the failure description.
    "on_step_finished(step_text: str, step_type: str, outcome: str)": |
      Close the step stack — the step context exits with the accumulated failure.

      `step_text`: the step sentence.
      `step_type`: the step type.
      `outcome`: the step outcome.

"EventLogFormatter(fmt: str, datefmt: str)":
  location: logging_setup.py
  annotations: |
    Console formatter of the prettyplay log events — renders human-readable events without payload
    duplication. A logging.Formatter subclass.

    `fmt`: the logging format string.
    `datefmt`: the date format string.

    Requirements:
    - The human-readable event set — step_results_declared, step_inputs, step_candidate_code,
      step_cached_code, step_captured — renders as the plain message (the message already carries the
      execution context); every other event keeps its context visible through the appended extras.
    - Pure rendering — no I/O.

    Use `conventions` for the logging rules.
  methods:
    "format(record: logging.LogRecord) -> message: str": |
      Render the record; when the record's event is one of the human-readable events, return the
      rendered message as is; otherwise append the sorted non-reserved record extras as
      " | key=value" pairs.

      `record`: the log record, including its structured context.
      `message`: the formatted console text.

"install_event_log_formatter()":
  location: logging_setup.py
  annotations: |
    Install the prettyplay event log formatter on the console.

    Algorithm:
    1. Set the prettyplay logger to INFO.
    2. Attach a stdout stream handler carrying `EventLogFormatter`.
    3. Idempotent — a repeated installation adds no duplicate handler.

    Requirements:
    - Web-test runs render prettyplay log events in the console, human-readable, without payload
      duplication.
    - Installed by the configure step of the web plugin (the plugins cell) — active whenever the
      plugin is, independent of allure.

    Use `conventions` for the logging rules.

